---
import Primer from '../../layouts/Primer.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

// Interactive relaxation figure: a tilted double-well E(x) = (x²−1)² + 0.35x, sampled at build time.
// Minima near x ≈ −1.05 (deeper) and x ≈ 0.96 (shallower); the reader drops a ball and it rolls to
// whichever valley is nearest, not always the deepest.
const RX = { XMIN: 92, XMAX: 680, XLO: -1.55, XHI: 1.45, YB: 214, YT: 46, ELO: -0.5, EHI: 1.8, X0: 0.55 };
const rxX = (x: number) => RX.XMIN + ((x - RX.XLO) / (RX.XHI - RX.XLO)) * (RX.XMAX - RX.XMIN);
const rxE = (x: number) => Math.pow(x * x - 1, 2) + 0.35 * x;
const rxY = (e: number) =>
  RX.YB + ((Math.max(RX.ELO, Math.min(RX.EHI, e)) - RX.ELO) / (RX.EHI - RX.ELO)) * (RX.YT - RX.YB);
const rxPts = Array.from({ length: 161 }, (_, i) => {
  const x = RX.XLO + ((RX.XHI - RX.XLO) * i) / 160;
  return rxX(x).toFixed(1) + ' ' + rxY(rxE(x)).toFixed(1);
});
const RX_CURVE = 'M ' + rxPts.join(' L ');
const RX0 = { x: +rxX(RX.X0).toFixed(1), y: +rxY(rxE(RX.X0)).toFixed(1) };
const RX_LEFT = +rxX(-1.05).toFixed(1), RX_RIGHT = +rxX(0.96).toFixed(1);
---
<Primer
  title="At Inference Time"
  eyebrow="A Primer on Matter Models · Chapter Five"
  deck="A trained potential gives you a cheap energy and cheap forces. This chapter spends them."
  running="Ch. 5 — At Inference Time"
  prev={{ href: url('/primer/how-one-gets-made'), label: 'How One Gets Made' }}
  next={{ href: url('/primer/what-mlips-keep'), label: "What an MLIP Keeps — and Can't" }}
>
  <p class="lead">
    The potential takes an arrangement of atoms and returns one energy and the force on every atom —
    the same two quantities a DFT calculation hands back, but in milliseconds instead of hours. That
    speed changes what you can do with the energy landscape of Chapter 1.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="num">§ 1</span>Relaxation: roll downhill until the forces vanish</h2>

  <p>
    Start with the simplest thing you can ask. You have a guessed arrangement of atoms (a candidate
    crystal, a molecule sketched by hand, a defect dropped into a lattice), and you want the stable
    version of it: the arrangement nature would actually settle into. On the landscape, "stable" means
    a valley floor. Nudge any atom and the energy goes up; that is what makes it a resting place.
  </p>

  <p>
    A valley floor is exactly where the slope is zero, so the force on every atom is zero. That gives
    you a procedure. Read the forces at your guessed arrangement. They point downhill, toward lower
    energy. Take a small step in that direction. Read the forces again at the new arrangement; step
    again. Each step lowers the energy, the forces shrink, and you coast to a stop at the bottom of the
    valley. This downhill walk is called <strong>relaxation</strong>, or geometry optimization, and the
    arrangement it lands on is the relaxed structure.
  </p>

  <figure class="fig" id="relax-fig">
    <div class="fig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 250" role="img" aria-label="A double-valley energy landscape. Drag a slider to place a ball, release, and it rolls downhill into whichever valley is nearest, which is not always the deeper one.">
        <text x="60" y="40" class="svg-small">energy</text>
        <path d={RX_CURVE} fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.5" />
        <line x1={RX_LEFT} y1="212" x2={RX_LEFT} y2="220" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1" />
        <text x={RX_LEFT} y="234" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">deeper valley</text>
        <line x1={RX_RIGHT} y1="150" x2={RX_RIGHT} y2="158" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1" />
        <text x={RX_RIGHT} y="128" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">shallower valley</text>

        <line id="relax-force" x1={RX0.x} y1={RX0.y} x2={RX0.x - 22} y2={RX0.y} stroke="var(--oxblood)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#relaxArr)" />
        <circle id="relax-ball" cx={RX0.x} cy={RX0.y} r="9" fill="var(--oxblood)" />

        <defs>
          <marker id="relaxArr" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="4.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L9 4.5 L0 9 z" fill="var(--oxblood)"/></marker>
        </defs>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="fig-controls">
      <span class="fig-ctl-label">start</span>
      <input type="range" min="-1.55" max="1.45" step="0.01" value="0.55" class="fig-slider" aria-label="starting position of the ball" />
      <span class="fig-readout" id="relax-readout">drag to place, release to let it roll</span>
    </div>
    <figcaption><span class="fig-num">Fig. 1 · drag it</span>Relaxation. Place the ball and release it: it steps in the direction the force points, downhill, until the force shrinks to nothing at a valley floor. That floor is the relaxed structure. Notice it settles into whichever valley it started nearest, which is not always the deeper one. Finding the lowest valley of all is a separate, harder search.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var fig = document.getElementById("relax-fig");
      if (!fig) return;
      var XMIN = 92, XMAX = 680, XLO = -1.55, XHI = 1.45, YB = 214, YT = 46, ELO = -0.5, EHI = 1.8;
      function X(x) { return XMIN + ((x - XLO) / (XHI - XLO)) * (XMAX - XMIN); }
      function E(x) { return Math.pow(x * x - 1, 2) + 0.35 * x; }
      function Y(e) { return YB + ((Math.max(ELO, Math.min(EHI, e)) - ELO) / (EHI - ELO)) * (YT - YB); }
      function slope(x) { return 4 * x * x * x - 4 * x + 0.35; } // dE/dx
      var s = fig.querySelector(".fig-slider");
      var ball = fig.querySelector("#relax-ball"), force = fig.querySelector("#relax-force");
      var out = fig.querySelector("#relax-readout");
      var raf = null;
      function place(x, rolling) {
        var px = X(x), py = Y(E(x));
        ball.setAttribute("cx", px); ball.setAttribute("cy", py);
        var g = slope(x), near = Math.abs(g) < 0.01;
        var dir = g > 0 ? -1 : 1, len = Math.min(Math.abs(g), 2) / 2 * 34 + 6;
        force.setAttribute("x1", px); force.setAttribute("y1", py);
        force.setAttribute("x2", px + dir * len); force.setAttribute("y2", py);
        force.style.opacity = near ? "0" : "1";
        if (!rolling) out.textContent = "start x = " + x.toFixed(2) + " · release to let it roll";
      }
      function roll(x) {
        if (raf) cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
        function frame() {
          for (var k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
            var g = slope(x), dx = -0.03 * g;
            if (dx > 0.05) dx = 0.05; if (dx < -0.05) dx = -0.05;
            x += dx;
            if (Math.abs(slope(x)) < 0.004) break;
          }
          place(x, true);
          if (Math.abs(slope(x)) > 0.004) { raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame); }
          else {
            var deep = x < 0;
            out.textContent = "settled in the " + (deep ? "deeper (left)" : "shallower (right)") + " valley — the nearest one, not always the deepest";
          }
        }
        frame();
      }
      s.addEventListener("input", function () { if (raf) cancelAnimationFrame(raf); place(parseFloat(s.value), false); });
      s.addEventListener("change", function () { roll(parseFloat(s.value)); });
      place(parseFloat(s.value), false);
    })();
  </script>

  <p>
    With DFT this was painful: every step needs a fresh energy and forces, every reading takes hours,
    and a relaxation can run for dozens of steps. With a potential each reading is milliseconds, so the
    whole walk finishes in a moment. And once one structure is cheap, a thousand are cheap. You can
    relax thousands of candidate arrangements and keep the ones that settle lowest. That is the engine
    behind screening new materials.
  </p>

  <aside class="sidenote">
    <span class="sn-label">One caution</span>
    Rolling downhill finds <em>a</em> valley, the nearest one, not necessarily the deepest. A landscape
    has many valleys, and relaxation stops at whichever floor your starting guess drains into. Finding
    the lowest valley of all is a separate and harder search; relaxation only takes you to the bottom of
    the basin you started in.
  </aside>

  <h2><span class="num">§ 2</span>Molecular dynamics: step the atoms through time</h2>

  <p>
    Relaxation finds where atoms come to rest. But atoms at any real temperature do not rest — they
    move, jostle, and trade energy. To watch that, you let them move under their own forces, one tiny
    instant at a time.
  </p>

  <p>
    The loop is short. Take the current positions and read the force on every atom. A force is a push,
    and a push makes a mass accelerate — heavier atoms respond less, lighter ones more. Over one very
    short slice of time (a femtosecond, a millionth of a billionth of a second) the forces set how
    each velocity changes, and the velocities set how each position changes. Call that slice one
    <strong>time-step</strong>. Step it forward and you have a new arrangement, a sliver of time
    later. Read the forces there and repeat. Step and repeat, step and repeat — this is
    <strong>molecular dynamics</strong>, and the path traced out step by step is a
    <strong>trajectory</strong>.
  </p>

  <figure class="fig">
    <div class="fig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 250" role="img" aria-label="A four-stage cycle: positions feed into the model, the model returns forces, forces update velocities and positions, and the loop repeats.">
        <rect x="280" y="20" width="160" height="40" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" />
        <text x="360" y="44" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-serif" style="font-size:14px;">positions</text>

        <rect x="500" y="100" width="170" height="46" fill="none" stroke="var(--oxblood)" stroke-width="1.6" />
        <text x="585" y="120" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-serif" style="font-size:14px;">the potential</text>
        <text x="585" y="137" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">→ forces</text>

        <rect x="280" y="186" width="160" height="44" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" />
        <text x="360" y="206" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-serif" style="font-size:14px;">update velocities</text>
        <text x="360" y="222" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-serif" style="font-size:14px;">&amp; positions</text>

        <rect x="50" y="100" width="170" height="46" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" />
        <text x="135" y="128" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">one time-step later</text>

        <line x1="440" y1="46" x2="512" y2="100" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#ch6arrB)" />
        <line x1="560" y1="146" x2="442" y2="196" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#ch6arrB)" />
        <line x1="280" y1="208" x2="150" y2="146" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#ch6arrB)" />
        <line x1="150" y1="100" x2="280" y2="46" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#ch6arrB)" />

        <text x="360" y="128" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-serif-it" style="font-size:13px;">milliseconds per step,</text>
        <text x="360" y="146" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-serif-it" style="font-size:13px;">millions of steps</text>

        <defs>
          <marker id="ch6arrB" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="4.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L9 4.5 L0 9 z" fill="var(--ink)"/></marker>
        </defs>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption><span class="fig-num">Fig. 2</span>The molecular-dynamics loop: from positions, the potential returns forces; the forces update the velocities and positions; one time-step later the loop runs again.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    This is the cheap mirror of the slow loop Chapter 1 described. There, each step asked DFT for the
    energy and forces, so a trajectory of any length was hopeless — millions of femtosecond steps are
    needed to see anything happen. Replace the DFT reading with a potential reading and the cost per
    step collapses. Now those millions of steps are routine: where DFT dynamics reaches tens of
    picoseconds on tens of atoms, a potential runs nanoseconds on millions. The impossible regime from
    Chapter 1 becomes an afternoon.
  </p>

  <p>
    One piece is missing. Left alone, the loop holds total energy nearly constant, but it leaves the
    temperature free to drift. To run at, say, room
    temperature, you attach a <strong>thermostat</strong>: a small adjustment, applied each step, that
    gently nudges the atoms' speeds up or down to hold the average kinetic energy at the value
    corresponding to the target temperature. Set the thermostat to 300 kelvin and the simulation runs as
    if the sample sat in a 300-kelvin room.
  </p>

  <p>
    With that, you can watch processes, not just resting structures. Heat a crystal past its melting
    point and see the lattice give way to a liquid. Track a single atom wandering through a solid over
    time — <strong>diffusion</strong>. Pull on a slab until a <strong>crack</strong> starts (a line of
    broken bonds) and watch that line advance one bond at a time as the front reaches each atom. Press
    two materials together and watch the interface between them settle.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="num">§ 3</span>Derived quantities: reading more off the surface</h2>

  <p>
    The landscape's local shape holds more than resting places and trajectories. Sit at a valley floor
    and ask how steeply the walls rise. That curvature (how fast the force grows as you push an
    atom off the bottom) sets how the atoms vibrate — the phonons; from the vibrations follow heat
    capacity and, for sufficiently anharmonic materials, thermal expansion. Squeeze the whole box a
    little and watch the energy climb: how stiffly it resists gives the elastic constants, the
    material's springiness. All of it is the landscape's local shape, sampled with cheap evaluations
    instead of expensive ones.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two quantities ask a little more. A chemical reaction is a trip from one valley to another over the ridge
    between them, and the height of the lowest pass sets how fast the reaction goes — the
    <strong>reaction barrier</strong>. Finding that pass is its own small search, the
    <strong>nudged elastic band</strong>. String a chain of intermediate arrangements between the
    two valleys, like beads, and pull every bead downhill. Springs between neighboring beads
    (bookkeeping forces, not physical ones) keep the chain from sliding into either valley, so it
    settles draped over the ridge, through the lowest pass. And a <strong>free energy</strong>, the
    quantity that decides which phase or arrangement is stable at a given temperature, is not read
    at one point but averaged over a stretch of dynamics at that temperature. Every one of these
    leans on the same cheap evaluation; that is what makes them practical.
  </p>

  <h2><span class="num">§ 4</span>Serving it: the drop-in calculator</h2>

  <p>
    Mature simulation tools already know how to relax a structure, run dynamics, compute phonons — the
    whole catalog above. The one thing they lack is the
    physics. They expect to be handed something that, given the current positions, returns the energy and
    the forces. They call that something a <strong>calculator</strong>, and they ask it the same question
    every step.
  </p>

  <p>
    That slot is exactly the shape of an MLIP. A potential is a function from positions to energy and
    forces; a calculator is an object that returns energy and forces from positions. Wrap the model so it
    answers to the calculator's two questions and it plugs straight into the simulation tool — for
    instance, the widely used Atomic Simulation Environment, or <strong>ASE</strong>. Your relaxation and
    dynamics code never learns which model is behind the slot, so you can swap one potential for another
    without touching the surrounding code.
  </p>

  <div class="cmd">atoms = read("candidate.cif")        # the arrangement
atoms.calc = MyMLIP(checkpoint="...")  # drop the model into the slot

relax(atoms)                          # tool calls the model each step
run_dynamics(atoms, temperature=300)  # so does this — same slot</div>

  <p>
    Installing a potential on a cluster (the right
    Python environment, the matching dependencies, the weights) is fiddly, and doing it once per model
    per machine is the kind of friction that keeps people from trying a second model.
    <strong>Rootstock</strong>, the backend behind these pages, pre-installs many potentials on shared HPC
    clusters and serves each through exactly this drop-in calculator interface. You pick a checkpoint, point
    your simulation at it, and the model runs on the cluster behind the same slot your code already speaks
    to.
  </p>

  <figure class="fig">
    <div class="fig-frame">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 230" role="img" aria-label="Three boxes left to right: the user's simulation code, a standard calculator interface, and the model running on a cluster, with positions flowing right and energy and forces flowing back left.">
        <rect x="40" y="80" width="160" height="64" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" />
        <text x="120" y="108" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-serif" style="font-size:14px;">your simulation</text>
        <text x="120" y="126" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">relax · dynamics · phonons</text>

        <rect x="280" y="80" width="160" height="64" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" />
        <text x="360" y="104" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-serif" style="font-size:14px;">calculator</text>
        <text x="360" y="120" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">the standard slot</text>
        <text x="360" y="134" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">(e.g. ASE)</text>

        <rect x="520" y="80" width="160" height="64" fill="none" stroke="var(--oxblood)" stroke-width="1.6" />
        <text x="600" y="104" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-serif" style="font-size:14px;">the model</text>
        <text x="600" y="120" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">on a cluster</text>
        <text x="600" y="134" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">(Rootstock)</text>

        <line x1="200" y1="100" x2="278" y2="100" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#ch6arrC)" />
        <line x1="440" y1="100" x2="518" y2="100" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#ch6arrC)" />
        <text x="360" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">positions →</text>

        <line x1="518" y1="126" x2="442" y2="126" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.1" marker-end="url(#ch6arrD)" />
        <line x1="278" y1="126" x2="202" y2="126" stroke="var(--ink-3)" stroke-width="1.1" marker-end="url(#ch6arrD)" />
        <text x="360" y="188" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-small">← energy &amp; forces</text>

        <defs>
          <marker id="ch6arrC" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="4.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L9 4.5 L0 9 z" fill="var(--ink)"/></marker>
          <marker id="ch6arrD" markerWidth="9" markerHeight="9" refX="7" refY="4.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L9 4.5 L0 9 z" fill="var(--ink-3)"/></marker>
        </defs>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption><span class="fig-num">Fig. 3</span>The serving pattern. Your simulation code asks the standard calculator slot for energy and forces; the slot is filled by a model running on a cluster.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2><span class="num">§ 5</span>Where it goes wrong at run time</h2>

  <p>
    The failure that bites is not a bad number on a single static structure. It is the trajectory.
  </p>

  <p>
    Run the dynamics loop long enough and the atoms wander somewhere the training set never went —
    a bond squeezed shorter than any the model was shown, a strange transient cluster. There the
    model extrapolates, silently, and the compounding Chapter 4 described takes over: a small wrong
    force makes a stranger arrangement, the stranger arrangement draws a stranger force, and a run
    that held for a million steps drifts off course or blows up. Every static test was passed; the
    arrangements that break the model were never in them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two habits guard against it. Keep the simulation in-distribution — run it on the kinds of systems the
    model was trained for, and be wary when it strays. And where a model offers an <strong>uncertainty
    estimate</strong> (a flag it raises when an arrangement looks unfamiliar), watch that flag and stop, or
    fetch real DFT for the strange region, before the drift compounds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fast, as long as you stay where the model has been. Keep that caveat and the trade is
    extraordinary: the energy of a set of atoms, once affordable only through hours of
    electron-solving, now runs forward across millions of atoms and millions of steps that DFT
    could never have reached. That is what a machine-learned potential is for.
  </p>

